  <section class="section" id="s1">
    <h2>1. What is Digital Personal Data?</h2>
    <p>Before we dive into laws and regulations, let us understand what we are actually trying to protect. <strong>Personal data</strong> is any information that relates to an identified or identifiable individual. The word "digital" simply means this data exists in electronic or binary form — stored on a server, transmitted over the internet, or processed by a computer.</p>

    <div class="analogy">
      <div class="analogy-title">Analogy — Your Digital Footprint</div>
      <p>Think of it this way: every time you sign up for an app, search something online, or make a UPI payment, you leave a trail of data behind — like footprints in sand. Your name, phone number, email, location, spending habits, browsing history, biometrics — all of it is your personal data. Digital personal data protection laws exist to ensure no one misuses those footprints without your permission.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Categories of Personal Data</h3>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Category</th><th>Examples</th><th>Sensitivity Level</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Identity Data</td><td>Name, Aadhaar number, PAN, passport</td><td>High</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Contact Data</td><td>Email, phone number, home address</td><td>Medium</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Financial Data</td><td>Bank account, credit card, UPI ID</td><td>Very High</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Biometric Data</td><td>Fingerprint, retina scan, face ID</td><td>Critical</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Health Data</td><td>Medical records, prescriptions, disabilities</td><td>Very High</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Behavioural Data</td><td>App usage, browsing history, location tracks</td><td>Medium–High</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Inferred Data</td><td>Credit score, political leanings, preferences</td><td>Medium–High</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>India generates <strong>over 7.5 billion data points daily</strong> from its 800+ million internet users. Without a robust legal framework, this data can be harvested, sold, or weaponised — enabling surveillance, discrimination, fraud, and manipulation.</p>
  </section>

