    <h3>Why Was It Needed?</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>The <strong>2017 Puttaswamy judgment</strong> by the Supreme Court declared <em>Privacy as a Fundamental Right</em> under Article 21, making a dedicated data protection law constitutionally necessary.</li>
      <li>India had no dedicated data protection law — only Section 43A of the IT Act, which was narrow and insufficient.</li>
      <li>The global digital economy needed India to align with international standards (GDPR in Europe, PDPA in Singapore).</li>
      <li>Rising cases of data breaches, identity theft, and commercial exploitation of personal data demanded legislative action.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Scope of the Act</h3>
    <p>The DPDP Act applies to:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Processing of digital personal data within India</strong> — regardless of where the data fiduciary is located.</li>
      <li><strong>Processing outside India</strong> — if the processing is done in connection with profiling or offering goods/services to persons in India.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="callout warning">
      <div class="callout-label">Important Exception</div>
      <p>The Act <strong>does not apply</strong> to personal data processed for personal or domestic purposes, data made publicly available by the individual themselves, or data notified by the government for research/national security under specific conditions.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

