  <section class="section" id="s3">
    <h2>3. Key Definitions &amp; Concepts</h2>
    <p>Understanding the DPDP Act requires mastering its terminology. Let us go through each core term with simple explanations:</p>

    <h3>Data Principal</h3>
    <p>The <strong>individual</strong> to whom the personal data relates. In simpler terms — <em>you</em>. When you register on Zomato, you are the Data Principal. The Act empowers Data Principals with rights over their own information.</p>
    <p>In the case of children (below 18 years) or persons with disabilities, the parent or legal guardian acts as the Data Principal on their behalf.</p>

    <h3>Data Fiduciary</h3>
    <p>The <strong>entity</strong> (person, company, or government) that determines the purpose and means of processing personal data. Think of them as the "steward" of your data — they have a fiduciary duty (like a trustee) to handle it responsibly.</p>
    <p><strong>Examples:</strong> Amazon India, HDFC Bank, a hospital chain, or the UIDAI (Aadhaar authority).</p>

    <h3>Significant Data Fiduciary (SDF)</h3>
    <p>A Data Fiduciary the government designates as "significant" based on factors like volume of data processed, sensitivity, national security implications, or risk to electoral democracy. SDFs face <strong>additional obligations</strong> like appointing a Data Protection Officer and conducting Data Protection Impact Assessments.</p>

    <h3>Data Processor</h3>
    <p>An entity that processes personal data <em>on behalf of</em> the Data Fiduciary. They act on instructions and do not determine the purpose. For example, a cloud hosting company that stores a bank's customer records is a Data Processor — they process data but the bank (Data Fiduciary) decides why and how.</p>

