    <h3>Right to Access Information (Section 11)</h3>
    <p>You can ask any Data Fiduciary to tell you: what personal data of yours they hold, how it is being processed, who it has been shared with, and the identity of all Data Processors they employ. This right transforms opaque data pipelines into visible, auditable processes.</p>

    <h3>Right to Correction and Erasure (Section 12)</h3>
    <p>You can ask a Data Fiduciary to:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Correct</strong> inaccurate or misleading personal data.</li>
      <li><strong>Complete</strong> incomplete data.</li>
      <li><strong>Erase</strong> data that is no longer necessary for the purpose it was collected, or when consent is withdrawn.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>This is sometimes called the <em>"Right to be Forgotten"</em> in global discourse — though the DPDP Act uses more measured language, balancing individual rights against legitimate processing needs.</p>

    <h3>Right to Grievance Redressal (Section 13)</h3>
    <p>Every Data Principal has the right to have their complaints addressed. Data Fiduciaries must establish a <strong>grievance redressal mechanism</strong> and respond to complaints within a prescribed period. If unsatisfied, the individual can escalate to the <em>Data Protection Board of India</em>.</p>

    <h3>Right to Nominate (Section 14)</h3>
    <p>A unique feature of the Indian law — you can nominate another individual to exercise your data rights on your behalf in case of death or incapacity. This is particularly forward-thinking for a country with 1.4 billion people and varying digital literacy levels.</p>

    <div class="callout success">
      <div class="callout-label">Practical Example</div>
      <p>Suppose a digital lender shows incorrect EMI data on your CIBIL report. Under Section 12, you can write to the lender demanding correction. If they ignore you within the prescribed timeframe, you can file a complaint with the Data Protection Board under Section 13.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

