  <section class="section" id="s5">
    <h2>5. Obligations of Data Fiduciaries</h2>
    <p>Rights for individuals translate into obligations for organisations. Data Fiduciaries carry the heaviest burden under the DPDP Act — they must act as responsible custodians of data entrusted to them.</p>

    <h3>Core Obligations</h3>
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num">1</div>
        <div class="step-content">
          <h4>Collect only what is necessary (Data Minimisation)</h4>
          <p>Like a doctor who only asks questions relevant to your health, a fiduciary can collect only the data required for the stated purpose — nothing more.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num">2</div>
        <div class="step-content">
          <h4>Obtain free, specific, informed, and unconditional consent</h4>
          <p>Consent must be granular — not buried in 50-page terms. Each purpose requires separate, clear consent. Bundled or coercive consent is invalid.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num">3</div>
        <div class="step-content">
          <h4>Ensure accuracy of data</h4>
          <p>Fiduciaries must maintain reasonable efforts to ensure data is accurate, complete, and updated — especially when used for decisions that affect the individual.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num">4</div>
        <div class="step-content">
          <h4>Implement security safeguards</h4>
          <p>Reasonable security measures must protect against breaches, unauthorised access, or data theft. Security is not optional — it is mandatory.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num">5</div>
        <div class="step-content">
          <h4>Notify breaches to the Data Protection Board and affected individuals</h4>
          <p>If a data breach occurs, the fiduciary must notify both the Board and the affected Data Principals promptly — with details of what happened, what data was affected, and remediation steps.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num">6</div>
        <div class="step-content">
          <h4>Erase data when purpose is fulfilled</h4>
          <p>Data must not be retained indefinitely. Once the purpose of collection is met and legal retention periods expire, data must be securely deleted — not just archived in a cold server forever.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>Special Obligations for Significant Data Fiduciaries</h3>
    <p>Entities designated as Significant Data Fiduciaries (SDFs) by the government face additional requirements:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Appoint a <strong>Data Protection Officer (DPO)</strong> — a senior person based in India, responsible for compliance.</li>
      <li>Appoint an independent <strong>data auditor</strong> to periodically assess compliance.</li>
      <li>Conduct <strong>Data Protection Impact Assessments (DPIAs)</strong> before launching new products involving large-scale personal data processing.</li>
      <li>Avoid algorithmic processing that creates risks of harm to Data Principals.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Penalties for Non-Compliance</h3>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Violation Type</th><th>Maximum Penalty</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Failure to implement security safeguards leading to breach</td><td>₹250 Crore</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Failure to notify breach to Board or Data Principal</td><td>₹200 Crore</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Non-compliance with additional SDF obligations</td><td>₹150 Crore</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Processing children's data in violation of Act</td><td>₹200 Crore</td></tr>
          <tr><td>General non-compliance with provisions</td><td>₹50 Crore</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="callout danger">
      <div class="callout-label">Critical Note</div>
      <p>These are <strong>per-instance</strong> penalties, not capped across all violations. A single large data breach could expose a company to penalties exceeding ₹250 crore if multiple violations are found simultaneously — making compliance not just a legal obligation but a critical business risk.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

