  <section class="section" id="s6">
    <h2>6. The Consent Framework</h2>
    <p>Consent is the bedrock of the DPDP Act. Unlike older frameworks where a long, unintelligible privacy policy counted as consent, the DPDP Act sets strict standards for what valid consent looks like.</p>

    <h3>Elements of Valid Consent</h3>
    <p>The Act requires consent to be:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Free</strong> — not obtained under pressure, coercion, or undue influence.</li>
      <li><strong>Specific</strong> — each distinct purpose must have a separate consent request.</li>
      <li><strong>Informed</strong> — the individual must clearly understand what they are consenting to.</li>
      <li><strong>Unconditional</strong> — consent cannot be bundled with service agreements ("take it or leave it" consent is invalid).</li>
      <li><strong>Unambiguous</strong> — must involve a positive, affirmative act (pre-ticked boxes are invalid).</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="analogy">
      <div class="analogy-title">Analogy — Medical Informed Consent</div>
      <p>Just as a doctor must clearly explain a surgical procedure, its risks, and alternatives before you sign a consent form — a Data Fiduciary must clearly explain the specific use of your data, in simple language, before collecting it. A blanket "I agree to terms and conditions" no longer suffices.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Withdrawal of Consent</h3>
    <p>Withdrawal must be as <em>easy as giving</em> consent. If you gave consent with one tap, the fiduciary must allow withdrawal with equal ease. Upon withdrawal:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The fiduciary must stop processing data for that purpose.</li>
      <li>Data collected solely for that purpose must be deleted.</li>
      <li>Consequences of withdrawal (if any, like losing access to a service) must be clearly communicated upfront.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Legitimate Uses Without Consent</h3>
    <p>The Act recognises that not all processing requires explicit consent. Data can be processed without consent for <strong>legitimate uses</strong> including:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Performing a function of the State or providing subsidies/benefits</li>
      <li>Compliance with a court or judicial proceeding</li>
      <li>Medical emergency or epidemic response</li>
      <li>Employment-related processing (with safeguards)</li>
      <li>Voluntary disclosure by the Data Principal</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Processing Children's Data</h3>
    <p>The DPDP Act provides heightened protection for children (under 18 years). Data Fiduciaries must:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Obtain <strong>verifiable parental consent</strong> before processing children's data.</li>
      <li>Not process children's data in ways that could be <em>detrimental</em> to their wellbeing.</li>
      <li>Not undertake <strong>tracking, behavioural monitoring, or targeted advertising</strong> directed at children.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The government can exempt certain fiduciaries (like educational institutions) from the parental consent requirement if the processing is clearly in the child's interest.</p>
  </section>

