  <section class="section" id="s7">
    <h2>7. Cross-Border Data Transfers</h2>
    <p>In a globalised economy, personal data routinely crosses borders — an Indian user's data may be stored on servers in the United States or processed in Singapore. The DPDP Act introduces a <strong>whitelist model</strong> for international data transfers.</p>

    <h3>The Whitelist Approach</h3>
    <p>Unlike Europe's GDPR (which requires an "adequacy decision" before data can leave the EU), India's DPDP Act takes a more flexible approach. The <strong>Central Government will notify a list of countries</strong> to which personal data transfer is permitted. Transfer to non-whitelisted countries will require additional safeguards or will be restricted.</p>

