  <section class="section" id="s8">
    <h2>8. The Data Protection Board of India</h2>
    <p>The <strong>Data Protection Board of India (DPBI)</strong> is the independent regulatory body established by the DPDP Act. It is the enforcement arm of the law — the body that investigates breaches, hears complaints, and imposes penalties.</p>

    <h3>Key Functions</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Adjudication</strong> — Deciding complaints filed by Data Principals against Data Fiduciaries.</li>
      <li><strong>Suo Motu Action</strong> — Investigating data breaches and violations on its own initiative (without a complaint).</li>
      <li><strong>Penalty Imposition</strong> — Levying financial penalties for violations within prescribed limits.</li>
      <li><strong>Appeals</strong> — Acting as the first appellate body before the High Court.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Structure &amp; Independence</h3>
    <p>The Board is designed as a <em>digital-first</em> body — proceedings are conducted electronically. Its members include a Chairperson and several Members, appointed by the Central Government in consultation with a Selection Committee. The Board has the powers of a civil court for certain purposes, including summoning witnesses and requiring production of documents.</p>

    <div class="callout">
      <div class="callout-label">Criticism &amp; Debate</div>
      <p>Civil society groups have raised concerns that the Board's members are appointed by the same government that is also a major Data Fiduciary. This raises questions about <em>structural independence</em> — the Board may face political pressure when adjudicating complaints against government data processing. The Act does not include judicial members, unlike some international equivalents.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>How to File a Complaint</h3>
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num">1</div>
        <div class="step-content">
          <h4>First Exhaust Fiduciary's Grievance Mechanism</h4>
          <p>Before approaching the Board, you must first file a complaint with the Data Fiduciary's internal grievance officer and allow the prescribed response period to pass.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num">2</div>
        <div class="step-content">
          <h4>File Complaint with the Board (Digital Platform)</h4>
          <p>Submit your complaint through the Board's online portal with supporting documents and the fiduciary's response (or lack thereof).</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num">3</div>
        <div class="step-content">
          <h4>Board Issues Notice &amp; Conducts Hearing</h4>
          <p>The Board issues a notice to the Data Fiduciary, which must respond. Hearings are conducted through video conferencing or other digital means.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num">4</div>
        <div class="step-content">
          <h4>Order Passed — Penalty or Compliance Direction</h4>
          <p>The Board passes a speaking order, which may include penalties, directions to take specific actions, or dismissal of the complaint.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

