  <section class="section" id="s9">
    <h2>9. Intermediary Guidelines 2021 — Overview</h2>
    <p>While the DPDP Act governs <em>personal data protection</em>, the <strong>Information Technology (Intermediary Guidelines and Digital Media Ethics Code) Rules, 2021</strong> — commonly called the <em>IT Rules 2021</em> or <em>Intermediary Guidelines</em> — govern the <strong>liability, conduct, and obligations</strong> of online platforms and intermediaries operating in India.</p>

    <p>These rules were issued under <strong>Section 87 of the Information Technology Act, 2000</strong> and replaced the earlier Information Technology (Intermediaries Guidelines) Rules, 2011. They represent the government's attempt to bring greater accountability, content moderation standards, and user grievance mechanisms to digital platforms.</p>

    <div class="analogy">
      <div class="analogy-title">Analogy — The Platform as a "Post Office"</div>
      <p>A post office is not responsible for the contents of the letters it delivers — as long as it doesn't open them or know they contain illegal material. Similarly, under older IT law, online intermediaries (platforms like Facebook or WhatsApp) were not liable for third-party content — as long as they were passive conduits. The 2021 Rules dramatically change this by requiring active moderation, traceability, and government cooperation.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Background — Why Were the Rules Updated?</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Explosive growth of social media — from 50 million to 500+ million Indian users between 2011 and 2021.</li>
      <li>Rampant spread of fake news, misinformation, child sexual abuse material (CSAM), and inflammatory content.</li>
      <li>Lack of accountability for platforms refusing to identify originators of harmful viral messages (e.g., WhatsApp during mob-lynching incidents).</li>
      <li>Absence of domestic grievance mechanisms — Indian users could not escalate complaints to local officers.</li>
      <li>Regulatory parity with global trends (EU Digital Services Act, UK Online Safety Act).</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Scope of the 2021 Rules</h3>
    <p>The Rules apply to two distinct categories:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Intermediaries</strong> — Any person or entity providing digital services through which third-party content is hosted, transmitted, or published (search engines, social media, e-commerce platforms, messaging apps, cloud hosting).</li>
      <li><strong>Digital Media Publishers</strong> — Online news portals and OTT (Over-The-Top) streaming platforms like Netflix, Hotstar, Zee5, etc.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="callout">
      <div class="callout-label">Note</div>
      <p>This module focuses primarily on <strong>Intermediary obligations</strong>, as they intersect most directly with the DPDP Act. Digital media ethics for OTT/news platforms, while part of the same Rules, are governed by a separate Part III framework.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

