    <h3>Category 2 — Significant Social Media Intermediaries (SSMIs)</h3>
    <p>An intermediary is designated an <strong>SSMI</strong> if it has <em>50 lakh (5 million) or more registered users</em> in India. This covers platforms like Twitter/X, Facebook, Instagram, WhatsApp, YouTube, LinkedIn, and Telegram.</p>
    <p>SSMIs face significantly enhanced obligations:</p>

    <h4>Mandatory Appointments</h4>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Chief Compliance Officer (CCO)</strong> — A senior Indian employee responsible for overall compliance. Personally liable for violations.</li>
      <li><strong>Nodal Contact Person</strong> — Available 24/7 for coordination with law enforcement agencies.</li>
      <li><strong>Resident Grievance Officer (RGO)</strong> — Must be based in India; handles user complaints with stricter timelines.</li>
    </ul>

    <h4>Content Moderation Requirements</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>Deploy <strong>automated tools</strong> to proactively identify CSAM and other notified categories of harmful content.</li>
      <li>Publish a <strong>monthly compliance report</strong> disclosing: complaints received, actions taken, and content proactively removed.</li>
      <li>Provide users an <strong>appeal mechanism</strong> — a Grievance Appellate Committee (added in 2022 amendment) was also proposed, though legally contested.</li>
    </ul>

    <h4>Traceability Mandate (Messaging Platforms)</h4>
    <p>Perhaps the most contested provision: SSMIs providing <strong>primarily messaging services</strong> (like WhatsApp) must be able to identify the <strong>"first originator"</strong> of a message when ordered by a court or competent authority. This was introduced to enable identification of those who spread dangerous misinformation or illegal content.</p>

    <div class="callout danger">
      <div class="callout-label">Constitutional Controversy</div>
      <p>WhatsApp challenged the traceability mandate before the Delhi High Court, arguing it fundamentally compromises <strong>end-to-end encryption</strong> and violates users' right to privacy (as upheld in Puttaswamy). The matter involves a direct tension between state security interests and individual privacy — one of the defining legal battles of the digital age in India.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Safe Harbour Protection — Section 79 of IT Act</h3>
    <p>The <strong>safe harbour</strong> protection under Section 79 of the IT Act exempts intermediaries from legal liability for third-party content — <em>provided they comply with the Intermediary Guidelines</em>. If an intermediary fails to follow the Guidelines (e.g., doesn't have a Grievance Officer, doesn't take down notified content), it loses this protection and becomes liable for content on its platform.</p>

