  <section class="section" id="s11">
    <h2>11. Intersection: DPDP Act &amp; Intermediary Guidelines</h2>
    <p>The DPDP Act and the IT Intermediary Guidelines are <strong>complementary but distinct</strong> legal frameworks. Understanding how they interact is crucial for any platform, lawyer, or compliance professional working in India's digital ecosystem.</p>

    <h3>Where They Overlap</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Grievance Mechanisms</strong> — Both require intermediaries/fiduciaries to have dedicated grievance officers. The DPDP Act requires a Grievance Officer for data protection complaints, while the IT Rules require one for content-related complaints. For major platforms, this may need to be consolidated or co-located to avoid confusion.</li>
      <li><strong>User Rights</strong> — The right to erasure under DPDP and the right to have content removed under IT Rules both require platforms to act within prescribed timelines. Coordinated processes are needed.</li>
      <li><strong>Accountability of Personnel</strong> — Both laws impose personal liability on designated officers — the Chief Compliance Officer (IT Rules) and the Data Protection Officer (DPDP for SDFs) can be personally penalised for non-compliance.</li>
      <li><strong>Government Access to Data</strong> — Both laws enable government and law enforcement access to user data under specific conditions, creating potential privacy risks that must be managed with transparency.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Where They Diverge</h3>
