  <section class="section" id="s12">
    <h2>12. Case Studies &amp; Real-World Application</h2>

    <div class="case-study">
      <div class="case-tag">Case Study 01 — Data Breach</div>
      <h3>AIIMS Cyberattack (2022)</h3>
      <p>In November 2022, the All India Institute of Medical Sciences (AIIMS), Delhi, suffered a devastating ransomware attack. The attackers encrypted patient records affecting nearly 40 million patients — including health data, appointment histories, and Aadhaar-linked records.</p>
      <p><strong>DPDP Act Analysis:</strong> Had the DPDP Act been in force, AIIMS as a Data Fiduciary would have been obligated to: (a) immediately notify the Data Protection Board, (b) inform affected patients, (c) face penalty proceedings potentially up to ₹250 crore for failure to implement adequate security safeguards. This case became a catalyst for accelerating the DPDP Act's passage.</p>
      <p><strong>Lesson:</strong> Security safeguards are not bureaucratic checkboxes — they protect human lives, especially in critical health infrastructure where data breaches can endanger patient safety.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="case-study">
      <div class="case-tag">Case Study 02 — Intermediary Liability</div>
      <h3>WhatsApp Traceability Challenge (2021–Ongoing)</h3>
      <p>WhatsApp (Meta) filed a lawsuit in the Delhi High Court challenging Rule 4(2) of the IT Rules 2021, which mandates that significant messaging intermediaries must identify the "first originator" of messages when asked by a court or competent authority. WhatsApp argued this was technically impossible without breaking end-to-end encryption and constitutionally invalid as it violated the right to privacy.</p>
      <p><strong>Analysis:</strong> This case crystallises the fundamental tension in digital law — between <em>state security</em> (preventing harmful content spread, mob lynchings, terrorist communication) and <em>individual privacy</em> (the right to communicate without surveillance). The Puttaswamy judgment itself acknowledged that privacy is not absolute and can be restricted for legitimate state purposes if proportionate.</p>
      <p><strong>Lesson:</strong> Technology architecture (encryption) is now a legal battleground. Engineers, lawyers, and policymakers must work together to find technically feasible, constitutionally sound solutions.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="case-study">
      <div class="case-tag">Case Study 03 — Children's Data</div>
      <h3>BYJU'S Data Practices</h3>
      <p>EdTech giant BYJU'S faced significant regulatory and public scrutiny over its data collection practices targeting minors. Reports surfaced about extensive collection of children's behavioural data, app usage patterns, and parental financial information — often without clear consent disclosures.</p>
      <p><strong>DPDP Act Analysis:</strong> Under the DPDP Act, educational platforms accessing children's data must obtain verifiable parental consent and are explicitly prohibited from behavioural monitoring or targeted advertising directed at children. Platforms like BYJU'S would need to completely redesign their data collection architecture for minor users.</p>
      <p><strong>Lesson:</strong> EdTech companies must treat children's data with extraordinary care. The Act does not allow "implied consent" for minor users — it must be explicit, verified, and parental.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="case-study">
      <div class="case-tag">Case Study 04 — Monthly Compliance Reports</div>
      <h3>Twitter/X's Compliance Reports</h3>
      <p>Following the IT Rules 2021, major SSMIs began publishing monthly compliance reports. Twitter (now X) submitted reports showing tens of thousands of accounts actioned each month in India — covering content categories like CSAM, non-consensual intimate imagery, terrorist content, and government-ordered removals. These reports revealed both the scale of harmful content and the inconsistency in enforcement.</p>
      <p><strong>Analysis:</strong> Monthly reports serve dual purposes — they demonstrate regulatory compliance and create a public accountability trail. Civil society researchers use these reports to track censorship patterns, assess proportionality of takedowns, and identify potential abuse of takedown orders.</p>
      <p><strong>Lesson:</strong> Transparency is the cornerstone of the digital trust ecosystem. When platforms report their actions transparently, it enables democratic oversight — a key check on both corporate and government power.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

